  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 129 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nAn upper-level trough currently located over Baja California will \ncause the ridge over our region to shift towards South Texas this \nweekend. The 250mb ridge axis will still stay over our area, only \npivoting rather than moving away completely, so temperatures will \nremain summerlike with rain chances staying low. The drier air from \nFriday will be replaced by more humid air over the weekend, causing \nheat indices to creep back up into the low 100s by early next week. \nSome weak filaments of vorticity are expected to glide along the \nnorthern periphery of the displaced ridge once it shifts far enough \nsouth. Low potential exists for this to produce some isolated \nshowers or storms over the Edwards Plateau and Hill Country Sunday \nand Monday. These disturbances are weak, so subsidence from the \nnearby ridge will still tend to prevail and rain chances are 20% or \nless.\n\nSizable changes are afoot in the weather pattern next week. The \nmajor player in this changeup is a strong trough forecast to move\nacross the western US early next week, with potential phasing of \nthe polar and subtropical jet streams as it approaches our area. \nThe strength of this trough will inject a plume of tropical \nPacific moisture into the southwestern US. Ensemble trends over \nthe past day have also increased the likelihood that moisture from\nHurricane Polo in the Pacific will be incorporated in this spread\nof moisture. The primary uncertainty with this trough is timing, \nas its evolution over the southwestern US on Tuesday varies widely\nacross models and is consequential to how quickly it advances \ninto Texas mid to late next week. A faster trough may be able to \nbring deep tropical moisture farther east into South Central \nTexas, with increased potential for heavy rains around Wednesday. \nA slower trough could cycle out Polo's moisture to the west, which\ncould reduce the rainfall potential for our area and delay the \nmost favorable period for rains later to Friday. In the 00Z \nensemble cluster analysis, the faster/slower trough scenarios were\nroughly evenly split between ensemble members. What models do \nagree on is that this will be a pronounced trough strong enough to\nclear out the ridge, bringing rain chances mid to late next week.\nThe trough is also strong enough to bring the first noteworthy \ncold front of the season, which will likely be a focusing \nmechanism for the rain potential should it arrive. The national \nblend of models currently has the probability of highs being below\n90F Wednesday and Thursday in the 50 to 70 percent range along \nand north of I-10. The timing of this front will also depend on \nthe progression of the trough, though for now it looks the current\nwindow of arrival is between late Tuesday to early Friday based \non the current ensembles. \n\nIt's a little too early to get into specifics regarding rain \namounts. However, any pattern that involves the spread of deep \nPacific moisture, with moisture contributions from a tropical \ncyclone, merits attention as a possible heavy rainmaker for South-\nCentral Texas. For the time being, the main message is that the mid \nto late week period next week will likely bring a change in the \nweather pattern and the first opportunity for widespread rains this \nfall. Stay tuned to the forecast for further updates.",
